    "title": "AI Firms Are Buying Old Books in Bulk — Then Shredding Them",
    "source_text": "AI companies are buying antique books in bulk, scanning them, and shredding the originals.\n A 404 Media investigation published 21 July 2026 identified ISBNdb as a broker marketing physical-book acquisition to AI developers at volumes of 1,000 to 1 million titles per order, with buyer anonymity and NDAs included. Pre-2022 print commands a premium precisely because it predates generative text — it is guaranteed to be human-written and free of AI slop. The destruction is a cost choice rather than a technical necessity: machines like the Treventus ScanRobot digitize fragile volumes non-destructively at 2,500 pages per hour, but cutting spines off is cheaper at industrial scale, and a 2025 US court ruling treated destructive scanning of legally purchased books as fair use. One caveat worth keeping: neither ISBNdb's marketing nor the public reporting ties a specific completed order to a named AI buyer.",
